{"task_id": "Mbpp/160", "solution": "def find_solution(a, b, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns integers x and y that satisfy ax + by = n as a tuple,\n    or returns None if no solution exists.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def extended_gcd(a_val, b_val):\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes gcd(a_val, b_val) and integers x, y such that a_val*x + b_val*y = gcd(a_val, b_val).\n        \"\"\"\n        if b_val == 0:\n            return a_val, 1, 0\n        \n        g, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b_val, a_val % b_val)\n        \n        x = y1\n        y = x1 - (a_val // b_val) * y1\n        return g, x, y\n\n    # Edge Case 1: Both coefficients are zero\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        return (0, 0) if n == 0 else None\n\n    # Edge Case 2: One coefficient is zero\n    if a == 0:\n        if n % b == 0:\n            # Equation becomes by = n. x can be any integer, choose 0.\n            return (0, n // b)\n        else:\n            return None\n    \n    if b == 0:\n        if n % a == 0:\n            # Equation becomes ax = n. y can be any integer, choose 0.\n            return (n // a, 0)\n        else:\n            return None\n\n    # General case: Both a and b are non-zero\n    # Use Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find g = gcd(a, b) and a particular solution (x0, y0)\n    # for ax0 + by0 = g\n    g, x0, y0 = extended_gcd(a, b)\n\n    # Ensure g is positive (standard GCD definition)\n    if g < 0:\n        g, x0, y0 = -g, -x0, -y0\n\n    # Check if a solution exists (n must be divisible by g)\n    if n % g != 0:\n        return None\n\n    # Calculate a particular solution for ax + by = n\n    # Scale x0, y0 by n/g\n    k = n // g\n    x_p = x0 * k\n    y_p = y0 * k\n\n    # Normalize the solution to obtain a specific result (e.g., smallest non-negative x)\n    # The general solution is x = x_p + (b/g)*t, y = y_p - (a/g)*t\n    # We find 't' to shift x_p into the range [0, |b/g|).\n    \n    b_prime = b // g # The coefficient for t in x's general solution term\n    a_prime = a // g # The coefficient for t in y's general solution term\n\n    # Calculate x_final in the range [0, abs(b_prime))\n    x_final = x_p % abs(b_prime)\n    \n    # If x_final is negative (only if b_prime is negative, and then x_p % b_prime gives a negative result),\n    # adjust it to be positive if abs(b_prime) is positive.\n    # Python's `%` with positive divisor `abs(b_prime)` will ensure `x_final` is in `[0, abs(b_prime) - 1]`\n    if x_final < 0:\n        x_final += abs(b_prime)\n\n    # Calculate the 't' adjustment needed\n    # (x_p + b_prime * t_adjust) should be x_final\n    # t_adjust = (x_final - x_p) / b_prime\n    # Integer division handles this, as (x_final - x_p) will be a multiple of b_prime\n    t_adjust = (x_final - x_p) // b_prime\n\n    # Calculate the corresponding y_final\n    y_final = y_p - a_prime * t_adjust\n    \n    return (x_final, y_final)"}
