{"task_id": "Mbpp/593", "solution": "```python\ndef remove_leading_zeros(ip_address: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes leading zeros from each octet of an IPv4 address string.\n\n    Args:\n        ip_address: The input IPv4 address string (e.g., \"216.08.094.196\").\n\n    Returns:\n        The normalized IPv4 address string without leading zeros (e.g., \"216.8.94.196\").\n        If the input is malformed (e.g., not 4 octets, or non-numeric octets),\n        the original string is returned unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    octets = ip_address.split('.')\n\n    # An IPv4 address must have exactly 4 octets.\n    if len(octets) != 4:\n        return ip_address\n\n    cleaned_octets = []\n    for octet in octets:\n        try:\n            # Convert each octet to an integer and then back to a string.\n            # This automatically removes leading zeros (e.g., \"08\" becomes 8, then \"8\").\n            # It also handles \"0\" correctly by keeping it as \"0\".\n            cleaned_octets.append(str(int(octet)))\n        except ValueError:\n            # If any octet is not a valid integer (e.g., \"abc\"), the IP is malformed.\n            # In such cases, return the original string as per design guidance for error handling.\n            return ip_address\n\n    # Join the cleaned octets back together with dots to form the normalized IP address.\n    return \".\".join(cleaned_octets)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(array1, array2, k):\n    # Ensure array1 is the shorter array to optimize the binary search range.\n    if len(array1) > len(array2):\n        array1, array2 = array2, array1\n    \n    m, n = len(array1), len(array2)\n    \n    # The binary search will determine `i`, the number of elements taken from array1.\n    # `j` will then be `k - i`.\n    # `low` represents the minimum possible `i`, and `high` represents the maximum possible `i`.\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(k, m)\n    \n    while low <= high:\n        i = (low + high) // 2\n        j = k - i\n        \n        # Determine the four critical values around the partition:\n        # L1: The element immediately before the i-th element in array1.\n        # R1: The i-th element in array1.\n        # L2: The element immediately before the j-th element in array2.\n        # R2: The j-th element in array2.\n        # Use -infinity and +infinity for boundary conditions when i or j is 0 or at array length.\n        L1 = float('-inf') if i == 0 else array1[i - 1]\n        R1 = float('inf') if i == m else array1[i]\n        \n        L2 = float('-inf') if j == 0 else array2[j - 1]\n        R2 = float('inf') if j == n else array2[j]\n        \n        # Check if the partition is correct. The k-th element is max(L1, L2).\n        if L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1:\n            return max(L1, L2)\n        elif L1 > R2:\n            # If L1 is too large, we need to take fewer elements from array1.\n            high = i - 1\n        else: # L2 > R1\n            # If L2 is too large, we need to take more elements from array1.\n            low = i + 1\n    \n    # This point should theoretically not be reached if k is valid (1 <= k <= m+n).\n    return -1 # Default return if no k-th element is found, based on implied constraints."}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "def armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    # An Armstrong number is equal to the sum of its digits each raised to the power of the total number of digits.\n    # This function checks if the given number is an Armstrong number.\n    if number < 0:\n        return False\n    \n    original_number = number\n    num_str = str(number)\n    num_digits = len(num_str)\n    \n    sum_of_powers = 0\n    temp_number = number\n    \n    while temp_number > 0:\n        digit = temp_number % 10\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** num_digits\n        temp_number //= 10\n        \n    return sum_of_powers == original_number"}
