{"task_id": "Mbpp/751", "solution": "def check_min_heap(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given array represents a min heap.\n    A min heap requires that every parent node's value is less than or equal to its children's values.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # An empty array or an array with a single element is considered a valid min heap.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Iterate only through potential parent nodes.\n    # In a 0-indexed array, parent nodes are from index 0 up to (n // 2) - 1.\n    for i in range(n // 2):\n        left_child_idx = 2 * i + 1\n        right_child_idx = 2 * i + 2\n\n        # Check left child\n        if left_child_idx < n:\n            if arr[i] > arr[left_child_idx]:\n                return False\n\n        # Check right child\n        if right_child_idx < n:\n            if arr[i] > arr[right_child_idx]:\n                return False\n\n    # If all parent-child relationships satisfy the min heap property, it's a min heap.\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/752", "solution": "def jacobsthal_num(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the nth Jacobsthal number using an iterative approach.\n    The sequence starts with J(0)=0, J(1)=1, and J(n) = J(n-1) + 2*J(n-2) for n >= 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    a, b = 0, 1  # J(0) and J(1)\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        next_jacobsthal = b + 2 * a\n        a = b\n        b = next_jacobsthal\n    return b"}
